## Headline findings for the reviewer

**The macro-signal.** Every 2026 trend reality-check (Fireart, Bubble, StudioMeyer, topright) converges on the same story: the AI-template baseline has flattened, so premium sites are pivoting to *tactile restraint* — sharp geometry, 1px hairlines, zero-blur depth, disciplined color. **The Property Card System is already on the winning side of the 2026 curve.** The research says don't buy "human" by softening; buy it by adding precision that proves a person decided.

**Where 2026 actually puts "human."** Real sites ditch the soft UI/pastel axis entirely and get warmth from **type contrast + micro-feedback + copy voice**: Mercury (serif display at hero scale on warm-bone, tabular mono numerals), GOAT Finance (humanist serif + mono + gold dot, "never shouts"), Adigiit ("warmth as material"). Monetization: tabular-nums mono data, tracked-mono index labels, one hero line-mask moment, formalized six-state micro-interactions at the existing 120-200ms budget.

**The single sharpest lever (free):** Linear's documented *interaction completeness* — every element gets all six states (default/hover/focus/active/disabled/loading). Most sources call premium UI "not a specific look, but a consistent level of crafted microstates."

**What to avoid, evidence-backed:** scroll-jacking and slow cinematic scrolls (NN/g disorientation + INP damage; explicitly fading in 2026), glassmorphism, neon dark-mode maximalism, page-wide kinetic type (StudioMeyer: "more polish than substance" outside one hero moment), fake live-data gimmicks, and heavy WebGL heroes (800kB-2MB JS before content).

**Uncertainty flagged explicitly** (8 items in the report) — notably: no strong evidence a flat-block corporate site reads "human" purely visually (type+voice carry it), the custom cursor is the single most *optional* technique, and the honeycomb should be one member of a "signal family" (hex / grid-lines / ticker) rather than a repeated stamp. I also noted the one-brand-surface rule (blue/gold/yellow/pink already populate the palette — new motion must live at opacity, not new hues).
